1. e4 {lesson: don't play a fancy prep move unless you know that's the move in the exact position, play your own chess logical move. Don't ignore opening principles, like not moving the same piece twice without a very good reason. My best games come from long maneuvering games rather than pure tactical brawls. } g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. f4 a6 5. Nf3 b5 6. Bd3 Bb7 7. e5 Nh6 8. a4 c5?! {I knew c5 was a move after a4 in some lines but I shouldn't have gone for this if I wasn't 100 percent confident. I knew b4 was also a vvery logical move and pretty much always the move after nc3} 9. dxc5 b4 10. Ne4 dxe5 11. fxe5 Ng4 12. O-O  (12. Bf4  Bxe4 13. Bxe4) Nxe5  (12... Nc6 {this would have given me an edge. not wasting 3 tempo on 1 knight, but developing quicker, restricting nd4 jumps. The difference is that the pawn can now be recaptured with another knight, and a knight is the best piece to put on e5. }  13. Qe2 Ngxe5) 13. Nxe5 Qd4+? {not great because I can't capture on e5 anyways with the queen due to nd6! and white wins the queen or the bishop}  (13... Qxd3  ) 14. Kh1 Bxe5 {I misevaluated this position, thought I was doing well with my 2 bishops pointing at the white king. But the critical factor is that my development is slow and I'm uncastled. }  (14... Qxe5  15. Nd6+ exd6 16. Re1 O-O) 15. Qf3 Qd5  (15... O-O! {I should have castled. I incorrectly evaluated that nf6 was bad for me, losing my important light squared bishop for the knight, I incorrectly evaluated the resulting position after nd7, thinking it was about equal because white had the bishop pair. }  16. Nf6+ exf6  (16... Bxf6 {this is the position I saw, and I was right to think it was equal after Bxf6, but I didn't even consider exf6 which would have given black a big advantage. }  17. Qxb7 Nd7) 17. Qxb7 Nd7 18. c6  (18. Qf3  Nxc5 {black wins a pawn. } 19. Be3 Qd6 20. Rad1 Nxd3) Nc5 19. Qe7) 16. Qh3 Nd7 {f5 was my first instinct, but thought the threaatw was better than the execution. In this case it wasn't.}  (16... f5! {the computer wants the immediate f5 and to put the knight on c6, solidifying the bishop on e5 and the pawn on b4. }  17. Ng5 Nc6 18. Be3  (18. Bf4  Bxf4) O-O-O  (18... h6 {I didn't go for f5 and h6 because I completely missed that the bishop defends the h8 rook so thought h6 was an empty threat. }  19. Nf3  (19. Bf4  hxg5 20. Qxh8+)) 19. Rad1) 17. Be3 f5 18. Ng5 Nxc5? 19. Bxf5! gxf5?? {back to back mistakes. I thought I could get away with it but completely missed nf3 at the end. I did consider Qxg2 but missed ne7 at the end of the line defending the bishop and thought I had to go bxb2. }  (19... Qxg2+  20. Qxg2 Bxg2+ 21. Kxg2 gxf5 22. Bxc5 Rc8 23. Bxb4 Rg8 24. Rxf5 Bf6 25. Kf3) 20. Qh5+ Kf8 21. Rxf5+ Kg8 22. Nf3 *

